WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.970
2006.

2
00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.550
Muhammad Yunus and Grameen
Bank, in Bangladesh,

3
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.230
share the Nobel Peace Prize.

4
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.210
The idea: small loans
for people banks ignore.

5
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:18.010
In 2018, microfinance institutions
reported 139.9 million borrowers,

6
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.780
80 per cent of them women.

7
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.530
Fairtrade tries something
else: a minimum price.

8
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.750
For washed Arabica coffee,
1.80 dollars a pound.

9
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.230
Do small loans and fairer prices
really lift people out of poverty?

10
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:33.410
Do they end it?

11
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.350
Or make no difference at all?

12
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.030
In this mission, you'll
read six randomised trials,

13
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.250
and the story of Andhra
Pradesh, India, in 2010.

14
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.770
Then you'll follow
Fairtrade from Garstang,

15
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.030
a UK town, to the farm.

16
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.930
Your mission: evaluate whether small
loans and fairer prices lift people out of

17
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:52.690
poverty.
